Embedded React: a View `cutout` that leaves a transparent hole in the framebuffer.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, after the patches listed before this
one there. Changed files: backends/software/renderer_backend.c,
bridges/quickjs/js/src/embedded-react/index.d.ts,
bridges/quickjs/native_ui_bridge.c, engine/core/native_renderer.c,
engine/core/renderer_internal.h, engine/include/er_scene.h,
engine/include/native_renderer.h, engine/scene/compositor.c and
engine/scene/er_node_internal.h. Not yet proposed upstream.

A host that draws GPU layers under the software framebuffer needs the
rasterizer to leave their rects see-through while still painting the UI above
them. A View with `cutout: true` now sets its clipped rect to transparent
(0x00000000) through a new optional backend callback, clear_rect, before its
background and children paint; the call is skipped in banded mode and inside
scratch captures. The software backend's source-over now composites alpha as
well as color, so content drawn over a cleared pixel keeps its own coverage
and is premultiplied for the host's blend. Over an opaque pixel the result is
bit-identical to before (alpha stays 0xFF), so frames without a cutout do not
change. A cutout node no longer counts as painting nothing.

diff --git a/backends/software/renderer_backend.c b/backends/software/renderer_backend.c
--- a/backends/software/renderer_backend.c
+++ b/backends/software/renderer_backend.c
@@ -60,26 +60,29 @@
  * @brief Composites one premultiplied-ARGB source pixel over an opaque destination pixel (Porter-Duff
  *        source-over).
  *
- * The framebuffer is kept opaque, so only the color channels are blended and the result alpha stays 0xFF.
+ * The framebuffer is opaque except where a cutout cleared it; alpha is composited like the colors, so an
+ * opaque destination stays 0xFF and a cleared one ends with the source's own coverage.
  *
- * @param[in] dst  Destination pixel (opaque ARGB8888).
+ * @param[in] dst  Destination pixel (premultiplied ARGB8888).
  * @param[in] sa   Source alpha (premultiply coverage).
  * @param[in] sr   Premultiplied source red.
  * @param[in] sg   Premultiplied source green.
  * @param[in] sb   Premultiplied source blue.
  *
- * @return The blended opaque ARGB8888 pixel.
+ * @return The blended premultiplied ARGB8888 pixel.
  */
 static inline uint32_t over_premul(uint32_t dst, uint32_t sa, uint32_t sr, uint32_t sg, uint32_t sb)
 {
     const uint32_t inv = 255U - sa;
+    const uint32_t da = dst >> 24;
     const uint32_t dr = (dst >> 16) & 0xFFU;
     const uint32_t dg = (dst >> 8) & 0xFFU;
     const uint32_t db = dst & 0xFFU;
     const uint32_t r = sr + div255(dr * inv);
     const uint32_t g = sg + div255(dg * inv);
     const uint32_t b = sb + div255(db * inv);
-    return 0xFF000000U | (r << 16) | (g << 8) | b;
+    const uint32_t a = sa + div255(da * inv);
+    return (a << 24) | (r << 16) | (g << 8) | b;
 }
 
